course: dictionary
lesson: 6059
title: A word from history
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A merchant is a person who buys and sells goods to make money. You can work out what it means from its context, the words around it. «The game shows only kings, generals and merchants.» Kings and generals are people in charge, so a merchant must be someone with a different job, and the clues point to buying and selling.
explain: Look for more clues in the next sentence. «Merchants sailed or travelled far to trade cloth, spices and gold.» Trade and goods are the clues. A merchant is a trader.
explain: Now swap your meaning into the sentence to check it. «A Venetian merchant called Marco Polo wrote a book about his court.» A Venetian trader called Marco Polo wrote a book about his court. It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A merchant is a person who buys and sells goods to make money, a trader.
fact: A merchant ship carries goods for trade, not soldiers.
fact: In history, merchants travelled far to trade cloth, spices and gold.
fact: In everyday life we say shopkeeper or seller more often than merchant.
exercise: «Marco Polo was a Venetian merchant who wrote a book about his travels.» What is a merchant? | *A person who buys and sells goods to make money | A soldier who fights in wars | A priest who teaches reading | A king who rules a large land
exercise: «Merchants of many peoples, like the Sogdians, carried goods both ways.» What did the merchants carry? | Soldiers and their horses | Stones for building castles | *Goods for trade | Books for schools
exercise: Which person is most like a merchant? | *A shopkeeper selling cloth in a market | A priest who prays | A king who rules a country | A general leading an army
test: «Kublai Khan became emperor of China, and a Venetian merchant called Marco Polo wrote a book about his court.» What is most likely true of a merchant? | A soldier who guards a castle | A person who rules a country | A person who cannot read | *A person who buys and sells goods
test: «Ideas travel like a football passed down a long line of players, reaching Europe through a merchant's son from Pisa.» Which clue helps you work out merchant? | The name Pisa | The football | The number of players | *Ideas and goods travelling from place to place
exam: «In farming societies eight or nine people in ten worked the land, while the game shows only kings, generals and merchants.» What does the contrast suggest about merchants? | They are soldiers who guard the kings | They are children who play games | *They are people who trade, unlike the farmers who work the land | They are farmers who grow food
